# --- MixTape (added) ---
# TypeScript incremental-build cache (emitted by `vue-tsc --build`) — machine-local, regenerated on every build.
*.tsbuildinfo
# Local-only / host-specific docs — never commit. (The host docs now live in
# docs/host.local/ below; this glob remains for any stray *.local.md.)
*.local.md
# Claude Code local/personal settings.
.claude/settings.local.json

# End-to-end run artifacts — all regenerated by `npm run test:e2e`, none of it source.
#
# The report and traces are large binaries (a trace bundles a DOM snapshot per step),
# and test-results/ is rewritten wholesale on every run. The saved session is a real
# authentication cookie for the seeded account — harmless against a throwaway sqlite,
# but a credential-shaped file has no business in a public repo. The database is the
# seeded throwaway itself, truncated at the start of each run.
#
# The GLOB covers sqlite's `-wal` and `-shm` sidecars, and the reason they are ignored
# rather than cleaned up is that they must NOT be cleaned up: the run deliberately leaves
# its database behind (the state a failure happened in is worth more than a tidy working
# directory — see globalTeardown), and uncheckpointed writes live in the write-ahead log,
# so deleting it would throw away the tail of exactly the run somebody wants to open. The
# next run removes all three before it migrates (resetDatabase in tests/e2e/support).
/playwright-report
/test-results
/tests/e2e/.auth
/storage/e2e.sqlite*
# The playable media the run generates: a copy of the committed one-second mp3 fixture
# at every path E2ESeeder claims, so the player specs stream real audio through the real
# stream route. Rewritten on every run (see seedMediaFiles in tests/e2e/support).
/storage/e2e-media
# Parked while a run is in flight; restored in teardown (see tests/e2e/support).
/public/hot.e2e-backup

# Home-infrastructure docs — never tracked (see CLAUDE.md -> Docs).
#
# This repo is public. docs/host.local/ holds everything specific to the one
# live server: hostname, LAN topology, MACs, the DynDNS host, the real domain,
# secret LOCATIONS. The generic, reproducible version of the same material is
# docs/self-hosting/, which IS tracked and uses placeholders throughout.
#
# The trailing slash ignores the whole directory. Note the *.local.md rule above
# would NOT cover files inside it (infrastructure.md does not match that glob),
# so this entry is doing real work — do not remove it.
#
# The directory is named generically on purpose: an ignore rule has to name its
# target literally, so a host-named directory would leak the host name into this
# tracked file — the exact thing the rule exists to prevent.
docs/host.local/

# Code-review working notes — never tracked, same reasoning as docs/host.local/ above.
# These are candid findings about a public, internet-facing codebase, and docs/ is
# curated documentation written for a stranger rather than a place for working notes
# (CLAUDE.md -> "DOCUMENTATION IS NOT AGENT MEMORY").
docs/review.local/

# Test-count plumbing for the README's badges, both machine-written and both regenerated
# on every CI run. `reports/` is the JUnit XML each suite is told to emit; `badges/` is what
# resources/build/testBadges.ts renders from it.
#
# The badge documents are PUBLISHED, but to a branch of their own rather than from here — a
# count committed onto main would put a number in the tree that every test run invalidates,
# which is the staleness the badges exist to remove. CI pushes the folder to `badges`, and
# shields.io reads it from there.
/reports
/badges

# `php artisan app:audit` writes its report next to wherever it was run, which is usually
# here, and `--cron` writes its baseline beside it. Both are throwaway working files —
# re-generated whenever you want them — and they would otherwise show up as untracked files
# in the changed-files view this project deploys from.
#
# ANCHORED to the repo root, and that is not tidiness: a bare `library-audit.md` matches at
# every level, which would silently exclude `docs/library-audit.md` — the documentation of
# the command that writes it.
/library-audit.md
/library-audit.state.json
